# Living Realms worldgen working map

## Purpose

This rule exists so Cursor does **not** need to rescan the whole repository for every worldgen task.

Start from the file map below. Read only the smallest relevant set of files first. Search outward only when a referenced symbol requires it.

The target is not merely “worldgen that compiles”. The target is **deterministic, complete, visually coherent starter civilization worldgen**.

## Product goal

For a fresh world:

- The 12 surface realms are strategically known before Living Realms authors any blocks.
- Capitals, towns, villages, hamlets, roads, gates, walls, farms, roadside sites, and Wizard Trees are day-zero worldgen fabric.
- When a Minecraft chunk finishes generation, all Living Realms fabric belonging to that chunk is already final.
- Nothing from the starter baseline should visibly appear later because the player flew closer.
- Later simulation growth, war damage, ruins, repair, expansion, and other evolving state may remain runtime systems.
- “100% worldgen” does **not** mean pregenerating every chunk of all kingdoms at world creation. It means the complete civilization plan is deterministic and each generated chunk is complete the first time it becomes playable.

## Non-negotiable invariants

1. **Fresh worlds only.** Do not spend engineering time on old-world migration or backward compatibility unless explicitly requested.
2. **No day-zero runtime construction fallback.** Starter physical civilization must not depend on player distance, chunk-load events, presentation radius, or tick-time construction.
3. **No half structures.** A multi-chunk structure must have one global accept/reject decision. Chunk A may not place half a house while chunk B independently rejects the rest.
4. **Chunk-order independence.** Generating chunks in a different order must produce the same physical result.
5. **Never force-generate/load neighbor chunks from worldgen.**
6. **No writes outside the chunk/worldgen region Minecraft currently allows.**
7. **Protect foreign content.** Never bulldoze vanilla/mod structures, block entities, or unknown non-natural construction.
8. **Terrain decisions happen before writes.** A building/bridge/wall must be planned against generator terrain before the first block of that object is authored.
9. **Roads are continuous surfaces.** No checkerboard/diamond holes caused by rounded side offsets.
10. **CITY+ boundaries have usable exits.** Gates, approach roads, internal streets, and regional routes must form one connected transport graph.
11. **No duplicate planners.** Reuse/extend existing manifest, settlement contract, street graph, boundary, and route planner instead of creating parallel competing truth.
12. **Keep worldgen bounded.** No unbounded A*, global terrain scan, or all-settlement blueprint materialization on the level-load/worldgen hot path.
13. **Do not trigger remote CI just to validate.** The user builds/tests locally. Keep changes easy to build with `build-local.ps1`.

## Known user-observed failures to eliminate

These are real in-game failures, not theoretical edge cases:

- Worldgen can visibly “catch up” behind the player while flying.
- Some houses/buildings are cut in half at chunk boundaries.
- Local paths can appear as a diamond/checkerboard mask with natural blocks between road cells.
- Regional water crossings can look like thin stone skeletons / isolated pillars rather than bridges.
- Walled settlements can have no obvious usable exit.
- Gate placement, internal roads, and regional routes can fail to read as one connected system.
- Some settlements feel like scattered buildings instead of a city/kingdom center.
- Terrain adaptation can create absurd walls/supports when the site should have been relocated or re-planned.
- Worldgen quality varies too much by terrain/seed.
- Houses are temporary quality-wise; the user plans to replace/improve the house library later. Keep architecture asset concerns separated from worldgen correctness.

