## Worldgen V2 implementation direction

Use this order unless code evidence shows a better dependency sequence.

### Phase 1 — Manifest and contracts
- Keep `StarterCivilizationManifest` as the strategic source audit.
- Expand `StarterSettlementWorldgenContract` until a planned settlement cannot silently be incomplete.
- Add explicit physical counts/connectivity checks needed for capitals/towns/villages/hamlets.

### Phase 2 — Resolve settlement sites once
- Resolve terrain position once per settlement.
- Derive one immutable physical settlement plan.
- Determine fixed-structure base Y / terrain viability before block writes.
- If an essential object is invalid, deterministically relocate/replan the object or settlement.
- Do not silently omit required fabric.

### Phase 3 — Immutable physical objects / structure pieces
- Convert fixed structures into globally accepted immutable objects with bounding boxes.
- Prefer Minecraft `StructurePiece`-style semantics for cross-chunk emission.
- Every chunk gets a clipped view of the same accepted object.
- Eliminate current per-chunk object acceptance.

### Phase 4 — Roads
- Convert each planned centerline into one cached continuous integer road mask.
- Roads must be chunk-order independent.
- Local roads, gate approaches, and regional roads should share compatible rasterization rules.

### Phase 5 — Bridges and terrain engineering
- Detect crossings before placement.
- Represent bridges/causeways/tunnels as explicit planned objects.
- Enforce grade, width, clearance, abutments, and support limits.

### Phase 6 — Gate-to-route graph
- Validate physical core -> gate -> regional route continuity.
- Make this a fail-fast contract, not a visual best-effort.

### Phase 7 — Performance
- Keep startup strategic planning cheap.
- Heavy terrain work must be bounded and cached.
- No chunk should solve an entire multi-kilometre network.
- Avoid repeated blueprint construction and repeated generator-height sampling.
- A generated chunk should not receive visible starter additions later.

## Definition of done

Do not call starter worldgen “done” until all of these are true on multiple fresh seeds:

- Fresh world reaches playable state without a worldgen stall.
- 12/12 surface realms exist in the manifest.
- Every realm has exactly one capital and valid towns/villages/hamlets in configured ranges.
- Every starter settlement passes `StarterSettlementWorldgenContract`.
- No half house/building at chunk borders.
- No wall chopped because an adjacent chunk rejected the same object.
- CITY+ has a complete boundary and obvious usable gate exits.
- Gate approaches connect to internal roads.
- Regional routes connect to intended settlement gates.
- Roads are visually continuous with no checkerboard holes.
- Water crossings are coherent bridges/causeways, not sparse stone skeletons.
- Terrain-fit failures replan/relocate instead of creating absurd vertical structures.
- Capitals read visually as capitals; towns as towns; villages as villages; hamlets as hamlets.
- Flying quickly through new terrain does not reveal starter buildings appearing later in chunks that were already complete.
- No forced neighbor chunk generation.
- Vanilla/mod structures and block entities remain protected.
- Wizard Trees remain valid.
- Starter baseline is not rebuilt by runtime simulation.
- Later growth/evolution still works as runtime behavior.

## Working style for Cursor

- Do not scan the entire repository before each task.
- Start from the “Task router” file list.
- Read symbol definitions only when needed.
- Make small, reviewable batches.
- Before editing, identify the invariant being fixed.
- Prefer deleting transitional behavior after its replacement is proven rather than maintaining two worldgen paths.
- Do not add compatibility machinery for old worlds.
- Do not hide missing required content behind fallback success.
- If an essential worldgen contract fails, fail loudly in development/logging rather than silently generating half a settlement.
